1) Application container 

--> This is the main container that runs your application.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2) Init container

--> An init container runs before the main application containers.
    It is commonly used to perform initialization tasks.
    Suppose your application requires a configuration file before starting. e.g. httpd.conf file creation , java conf file creation 
    Database initialization
    Downloading configuration
    Setting permissions
    Preparing files

--> If the init container fails: then application does not start

spec:
  initContainers:
    - name: init-config
      image: busybox
      command:
        - sh
        - -c
        - echo "configuration" > /config/app.conf

  containers:
    - name: application
      image: myapp:1.0

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3) Sidecar container

--> A sidecar container runs alongside the main application container in the same Pod.

--> They can share:
    - Network
    - Volumes
    - Pod lifecycle

--> Common sidecar uses
    - Log collection
    - Monitoring
    - Proxy
    - Security agent
    - Configuration synchronization
    - Service mesh functionality

spec:
  containers:

    - name: application
      image: myapp:1.0

    - name: logging
      image: log-collector:1.0

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4) Ambassador container

--> An ambassador container acts as a proxy between the application and an external service.
    For example, your application needs to communicate with a database.

                        Application
                             │
                             ▼
                        Ambassador
                             │
                             ▼
                        Database

    The ambassador can handle things such as:
    - Proxying
    - Connection management
    - Routing
    - Authentication
    This pattern is less commonly discussed in modern OpenShift deployments than sidecars, but it is an important container design pattern.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5) Adapter container

--> An adapter container modifies or transforms the output of the main application so another system can understand it.

    Adapters are useful when:
    - An application cannot be modified
    - An external system expects a particular format
    - Metrics/logs need transformation

    Suppose your application produces:
    Application logs:
    INFO user=123 operation=login
    
    An adapter could transform that into a format expected by a monitoring system.
    For example:
    {
      "user": 123,
      "operation": "login",
      "event": "login"
    }


---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

6) Privileged Container 

--> OpenShift also supports containers that run with elevated Linux privileges, subject to OpenShift security controls.

            Normally:
            Container
               ↓
            Limited privileges
            
            A privileged container may have significantly more access:
            Container
               ↓
            Elevated privileges
               ↓
            Host resources

    For example, some infrastructure or node-level operations may require privileged access.
    However, you should avoid privileged containers unless they are actually required because they increase security risk.
    OpenShift uses Security Context Constraints (SCCs) to control what Pods/containers are allowed to do.
    
    Examples include controls around:
    - Running as root
    - Linux capabilities
    - Host networking
    - Host filesystem access
    - Privileged mode
    - SELinux context

7) Containers sharing a Pod

This is extremely important in OpenShift.
A Pod can contain one or multiple containers.

All these containers share the Pod's network namespace.
Therefore, they can communicate using:
localhost
They can also share volumes.

8) Ephemeral containers

--> An ephemeral container is primarily used for troubleshooting a running Pod.

    Suppose you have:
    Pod
    │
    └── Application container
    
    The application container might not contain debugging tools such as:
    bash
    curl
    ps
    netstat

--> Instead of rebuilding the application image, you can attach an ephemeral debugging container.
    This is particularly useful for troubleshooting production applications.

9) OpenShift build container

--> OpenShift can also use containers as part of the image-building process.

    The build environment itself runs in containers.
    Depending on your OpenShift setup, you may encounter:
    - BuildConfigs
    - Build pods
    - Source-to-Image (S2I)
    - Dockerfile/Containerfile builds
    - Pipeline-based builds


Quick comparison
Type	                                Main purpose	                                        Runs when?
Application container	               Runs application	                                    Normal Pod lifecycle
Init container	                       Initialization/setup	                                Before app containers
Sidecar	                               Supporting application	                            Alongside application
Ambassador	                           Proxy/communication	                                Alongside application
Adapter	                               Transform output/data	                            Alongside application
Privileged container	               Requires elevated privileges	                        As permitted by SCC
Ephemeral container	                   Debugging	                                        Added to running Pod
Build container	                       Build container images	                            During build
